On Thu, 2014-05-22 at 11:31 -0700, Víctor Hugo Gutiérrez Vélez wrote:
> Dear INLA team,
>
> I am working on the implementation of a model with nonlinear
> interactions in INLA such as:
>
> formula= z~f(x, y, model="rw2")
>
> When I plot the effects, I visualize an overall non-linear possitive
> slope. This is the exact opposite to what I would expect based on my
> exploratory analysis and the nature of the phenomenon. More
> confusingly the trend is the opposite to what I obtain when I run a
> linear interaction either as:

Hi, the formulation f(x,y,model="rw2") treats y[i] as weights in the
i'th linear predictor eta[i], so that eta[i] gets a contribution
y[i]*spline(x[i]), where  the spline is smooth/rw2 and y is fixed.
since rw2 is intrinsic, then the spline sums to zero.

> formula=x*y

this formula will be expanded into x+y+x:y, which contain terms not
covered by f(x,y,model="rw2")

> or as
> w= x*y
> formula= z~ w

which is again different from the spline-formulation.

> I would deeply appreciate any feedback to understand this outcome as I
> haven't been able to find any useful information to help me overcome
> this situation.

so the three formulations are all different and will not give the same
results, the closest is z~w and z~x*y, which is nested since z~w is the
same as z~x:y

I do not know what you try to achive, it could just be a confusion of
what the models really do. Maybe it will help setting up an simulation
experiment with a known truth.

Although the spline model seems non-linear, the model is linear in the
spline-cofefficients, so a truly non-linear interaction is not what you
get.

Best
H

